# Development Workflow

The project follows an Agile Scrum methodology and uses Jira to manage tasks, progress, and sprint activities.

## Workflow

1. Create or assign a Jira work item.
2. Review requirements and acceptance criteria.
3. Move the Jira work item to **In Progress**.
4. Pull the latest version of the repository.
5. Implement the required functionality.
6. Test functionality locally.
7. Commit changes with descriptive commit messages.
8. Push changes to the main branch.
9. Update the Jira work item with implementation details.
10. Move the work item to **Done** when all acceptance criteria have been met.

---

# Coding Standards

## General Guidelines

- Write clean and maintainable code.
- Follow the Single Responsibility Principle.
- Avoid duplicated logic.
- Keep methods focused on one responsibility.
- Prefer readability over complexity.
- Use meaningful names for variables, methods, and classes.
- Remove unused code and imports before committing.

---

## Naming Conventions

### Classes

```java
UserService
TicketController
ReportGenerator
NotificationService
```

### Methods

```java
createTicket()
assignTicket()
generateReport()
sendNotification()
```

### Variables

```java
userId
ticketStatus
assignedWorker
reportData
```

### Constants

```java
MAX_FILE_SIZE
DEFAULT_PRIORITY
EMAIL_TIMEOUT
```

---

## Formatting Standards

- Use consistent indentation.
- Keep code properly formatted.
- Group related methods logically.
- Maintain clear spacing between sections.
- Use IDE formatting tools when available.

---

# Commenting Standards

Comments should explain:

- Business rules
- Complex logic
- Architectural decisions
- Non-obvious implementations

Example:

```java
// calculate average completion time for completed tickets
```

Avoid redundant comments.

Bad example:

```java
// increment counter
counter++;
```

---

# API Development Standards

## REST Principles

Use resource-oriented endpoints.

Examples:

```http
GET    /ticket
GET    /ticket/{id}
POST   /ticket
PUT    /ticket/{id}
DELETE /ticket/{id}
```

## Response Standards

All endpoints should:

- Use appropriate HTTP status codes.
- Provide meaningful error messages.
- Validate incoming requests.
- Return structured response objects.

---

# Database Standards

- Use meaningful table names.
- Maintain foreign key relationships.
- Avoid duplicate data.
- Use indexes where appropriate.
- Validate data before persistence.
- Follow consistent naming conventions.

---

# Git Workflow

The project uses a single-branch development approach.

## Branch Strategy

All development is performed directly on:

```text
main
```

Team members are expected to:

- Pull before starting work.
- Push frequently.
- Communicate planned changes.
- Resolve merge conflicts promptly.
- Test changes before pushing.

---

## Commit Message Standards

Commit messages should clearly describe the implemented change.

### Good Examples

```text
Implement profile page API

Add team report generation

Fix ticket assignment validation

Improve email notification service
```

### Poor Examples

```text
Update

Fix

Work done

Changes
```

### Recommended Format

```text
KAN-109 Implement profile page backend functionality
```

---

# Contribution Guidelines

## Before Development

- Review the Jira work item.
- Understand requirements and acceptance criteria.
- Confirm any dependencies.
- Pull the latest repository changes.

## During Development

- Follow project coding standards.
- Commit regularly.
- Keep commits focused on a single task.
- Update documentation when required.
- Test functionality before committing.

## Before Pushing Changes

Verify that:

- The project builds successfully.
- Functionality operates as expected.
- Existing features remain unaffected.
- No debugging code remains.
- Documentation is updated if necessary.
- Acceptance criteria have been satisfied.

---

# Code Review Process

Since development is performed directly on the main branch, reviews are conducted through team collaboration rather than pull requests.

## Review Activities

- Jira work item discussions
- Team meetings
- Sprint reviews
- Demonstrations
- Code walkthroughs

## Review Checklist

### Functionality

- Requirements implemented correctly
- Acceptance criteria satisfied
- No regressions introduced

### Code Quality

- Readable code
- Consistent naming conventions
- Logical structure
- No unnecessary complexity

### Testing

- Functionality tested
- Edge cases considered
- Existing features remain operational

### Security

- Input validation present
- Authorization rules enforced
- Sensitive information protected

---

# Testing Standards

## Unit Testing

Verify individual components operate correctly.

Areas include:

- Service methods
- Business logic
- Utility functions

---

## Integration Testing

Verify communication between:

- Frontend
- Backend
- Database
- Email services

---

## System Testing

Verify complete user workflows.

Examples:

- Create ticket
- Assign worker
- Complete ticket
- Generate reports
- Manage organisations

---

## User Acceptance Testing

Verify that:

- Requirements are met
- Workflows operate correctly
- Users can complete intended tasks
- Stakeholder expectations are satisfied

---

# Definition of Done

A Jira work item is considered complete when:

- Functionality has been implemented.
- Acceptance criteria have been satisfied.
- Code follows project coding standards.
- Testing has been completed successfully.
- Documentation has been updated where necessary.
- No critical defects remain.
- Changes have been committed and pushed to the main branch.
- The Jira work item has been updated and marked as **Done**.

---

# Security Practices

The project follows the following security principles:

- Validate all user input.
- Implement role-based authorization.
- Protect sensitive information.
- Store credentials securely.
- Prevent unauthorized access.
- Use secure communication protocols.
- Log important security-related events.

